CRY HAVOC
Medieval man-to-man hex skirmish
Original 1981 (Standard Games) · 2026 remaster (Footsore / Gary Chalk)

TYPE
Tactical wargame. One counter = one named person or animal.
Scale: 1 hex ≈ 2 m. 1 turn ≈ 5–15 seconds.
Players: 2+. Play time: 1–3 hours. Complexity: low–medium.

MAPS
Hex grid, large hexes (≈2.5 cm).
Original pair: The Village, The Crossroads (A2 / 40×60 cm).
Later / remaster extras: The Forest, The Pasture, The Wooded Vale.
Terrain types: open, slope, scrub, trees, buildings, river, wall, road.
Terrain cost: 1 MP open; 2 MP slope/scrub/trees (often impassable to horse); higher for windows/doors.

UNITS / TYPES
Peasants, yeomen, billmen, sergeants, men-at-arms, squires, knights (foot + mounted), archers, crossbowmen, pack animals.
Each character unique (name, costume, values). Color-coded sides.

COUNTER STATES (variables)
Healthy / Stunned / Wounded / Dead
Mounted characters use extra counters (rider + horse / riderless / dead horse).
Printed values on counter:
  Attack (melee / weapon)
  Defence (armour)
  Movement Points (MP) — blue number
Typical MP (healthy): unarmoured foot 8 · armoured infantry 6 · dismounted knight 4 · unarmoured horse 15 · armoured horse 12
Wound ≈ halves values. Stun = minimal defence, inactive one turn.

SEQUENCE (fundamentals)
Phased turn (original):
  1. Shortbow fire
  2. Crossbow fire (except those already in melee)
  3. Movement (crossbows that fired cannot move)
  4. Second shortbow fire
  5. Melee
  6. Recover stunned
Missile range bands + CRT. Melee: attack vs defence ratio → d10 / CRT.
Results: retreat, stun, wound, kill. Terrain / facing / flank modifiers apply.
Stacking: normally 1 living character per hex. Dead bodies remain and impede.

VICTORY
Scenario-driven: kill / capture opponents, control location, or ransom.
Dead counters stay on map.

COMPATIBILITY
2026 remaster uses original rules + new Chalk art. Maps and counters compatible with 1981 edition.
